\DOC constructors_distinct_convs

\TYPE {constructors_distinct_convs : thm -> (term * conv) list}

\SYNOPSIS
Produces conversions proving that different constructors of an inductive type
give different values.

\DESCRIBE
{constructors_distinct_convs} takes as its argument a primitive recursion
theorem, in the form returned by {define_type}, just like
{prove_constructors_distinct}. It returns a list of pairs, one for each
constructor {C} of each type in the theorem that has at least two
constructors: a pattern {`C x1 ... xn = y`}, where {x1}, ..., {xn} and {y} are
(generated) variables, and a conversion. When applied to an equation
{`C s1 ... sn = D t1 ... tm`}, where {D} is a different constructor of the same
type, at any instance of the type, the conversion returns the theorem
{|- C s1 ... sn = D t1 ... tm <=> F}. The pairs are in the form expected by
{net_of_conv}, and {extend_rectype_net} uses them in this way to extend
{basic_rectype_net} and {constructors_distinct_net} when a type is defined.

The conversions for a recursion theorem all rely on one theorem numbering the
constructors, which {constructors_distinct_convs} proves at once, in time
roughly linear in the size of the recursion theorem. The theorems for {C}
against all the other constructors of its type are proved the first time the
conversion for {C} is used, and then saved.

\FAILURE
Fails if the argument is not a theorem of the form returned by {define_type},
or if no type in it has more than one constructor. The conversions fail unless
the term is an equation between applications of two different constructors of
the type.

\EXAMPLE
For a type of labelled binary trees:
{
  # let ith,rth = define_type "tree = LEAF num | NODE tree tree";;
  val ith : thm =
    |- !P.
           (!a. P (LEAF a)) /\ (!a0 a1. P a0 /\ P a1 ==> P (NODE a0 a1))
           ==> (!x. P x)
  val rth : thm =
    |- !f0 f1.
           ?fn.
               (!a. fn (LEAF a) = f0 a) /\
               (!a0 a1. fn (NODE a0 a1) = f1 a0 a1 (fn a0) (fn a1))

  # let cnvs = constructors_distinct_convs rth;;
  val cnvs : (term * conv) list =
    [(`LEAF _120602 = _120601`, <fun>);
     (`NODE _120604 _120605 = _120603`, <fun>)]
}
\noindent the first conversion proves distinctness for equations with {LEAF}
on the left:
{
  # snd(hd cnvs) `LEAF 1 = NODE t t`;;
  val it : thm = |- LEAF 1 = NODE t t <=> F

  # snd(hd cnvs) `LEAF 1 = LEAF 2`;;
  Exception: Failure "constructors_distinct_convs".
}

\COMMENTS
This function is mainly intended for internal use. The conversions for all the
types defined so far are available through {CONSTRUCTORS_DISTINCT_CONV}.

\SEEALSO
basic_rectype_net, CONSTRUCTORS_DISTINCT_CONV, constructors_distinct_net,
define_type, distinctness, extend_rectype_net, net_of_conv,
prove_constructors_distinct.

\ENDDOC
